It was always intended to be an ongoing project.  It's temporarily
paused right now because a lot of my recent work has been in C#, and
switching between pre-ANSI C and modern stuff is sufficiently annoying
that I've put things on hold for the time being.

It was a good time to pause it, simply because it was in a relatively
stable state; that makes it a lot easier to come back and say "OK, what
is the state of affairs now?"  Plus, to be frank, it needed some time
available to be played without me changing the 'rules of the game', so
to speak, constantly.

There have been times when I've considered putting out the cattle call
and taking up the project-manager reins as well as the one-guy-coding
reins, but that requires me to commit to a time investment that I'm not
sure I want to wander down just yet.  Nethack, while very popular, is
still a "classic" game, and I would be far more interested in, say,
porting it to the Nintendo DS (along with all the proper interface
mods).  That gets it on a "go-forward" platform, in front of all the
appropriate youthful eyeballs, and gives it a new lease on life.  This
also gives the project a better chance of picking up developers, since
you're working with modern new toys.

Brett Kosinki is working on a direct DS port, and he has informed me that
in fact Spork does apply properly to that port and will run.  To me,
this would be a far better approach than simply rehashing Nethack or
Spork in (foo new language) -- or even continuing Spork excessively in
its current set of platforms.

> I'm wondering whether the death (or deep sleep?) of vanilla Nethack
> and Slashem is because of the poor design and code and that authors
> get discouraged by it.  If that's the case, then the only hope is a
> rewrite.  On the other hand, the Linux kernel is an excellent piece of
> code, and it would be insane to rewrite it just because it's C.

I haven't particularly noted that Nethack was poorly coded or designed;
it's good to keep in mind that a number of its practices are standard
for pre-ANSI C and have been inherited over the years as such under the
"it ain't broke, don't fix it" rule.  Its biggest problem is that it is
a 20-year old codebase which many, many people have patched and worked
on over the years, and as such has a lot of jerky discontinuity when
you're trying to follow through it.  That can be disconcerting for some
coders, especially newer ones.

